answer(55,p('Use g = GM/R² and divide each result by Earth’s g = 9.81 N/kg.')+table(['Location','g (N/kg)','g / gEarth'],[('Moon','1.62','0.165'),('Mercury','3.31','0.338'),('Ganymede','1.47','0.150'),('Sun’s surface','273','27.9')])+p('Among the Moon, Ganymede, and Mercury, your weight is greatest on <strong>Mercury</strong>, since F<sub>g</sub> = mg.'))
answer(56,ANSWERS['q-18'])
answer(57,p('<strong>The question supplies Mars’s mass but omits its radius.</strong> Using R = 3.43 × 10⁶ m from section 3.1 Review question 8:','F = (6.67 × 10⁻¹¹)(6.4 × 10²³)(5.0 × 10¹)/(3.43 × 10⁶)² = 181.4 N ≈ <strong>1.8 × 10² N toward Mars’s centre</strong>. Without a radius or local g, the numerical force is undetermined.'))
answer(58,p('At constant speed, F<sub>fr</sub> = 0.525 N. For a horizontal pull, N = 5.00 N.','μ<sub>k</sub> = 0.525/5.00 = <strong>0.105</strong>.'))
answer(59,parts('Each crate contributes the same weight. Stacking two doubles N, so the required force is <strong>2(224) = 448 N</strong>.','Side by side, each crate still contributes 224 N of friction. Total force is <strong>448 N</strong>. The greater apparent contact area does not change the model’s result.'))
